WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.670
So it should be clear how
important genetic analysis is for

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.210
reconstructing evolutionary trees.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.090
Yet it has not always been so clear.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.560
When Zuckerkandl and Pauling wrote, half a
century ago, that hemoglobin offered a good

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.530
basis for comparing humans to gorillas,
prominent biologist Gaylord Simpson

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:26.060
shot them down in impressive fashion,
saying that their proposal was nonsense.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.890
Now today, Simpson lies on
the wrong side of history, but

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.040
at the time, perhaps Zuckerkandl and
Pauling were the radicals.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:41.020
Since that time, genetic analysis has
been applied to answer a huge number

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.610
of biological questions related to
evolutionary tree reconstruction.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:51.510
For example, until the 1980s,
it was unclear whether the giant panda

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.480
should be classified as a bear or
as a RACCOON (not marsupial).

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.180
The giant panda looks like
a bear in some ways, but

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.169
it's also similar to
raccoons in other ways.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.744
The panda doesn't hibernate in the winter,
and its male genitalia are tiny and

00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:10.240
backward-pointing.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.900
I'm going to let you insert
your own joke on that one.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.890
In 1938, Edwin Colbert wrote,
"so the quest has stood for

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.810
many years with the bear proponents, and
the raccoon adherents, and the middle of

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.810
the road group advancing their several
arguments with the clearest of logic.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:27.730
While in the meantime,

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:32.630
the giant panda lives serenely in the
mountains of Sichuan with never a thought

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.440
about the zoological controversies
he is causing by just being himself."

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.290
Now, whereas using anatomical or

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.960
behavioral characters led to
endless debate in this subject,

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.980
Steve O'Brien used genetic data in 1985 to
demonstrate that the giant panda should,

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.810
in fact, be considered a bear - 
a conclusion that has lasted to this day.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.030
We can also turn genetic analysis
inward onto ourselves, and

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.110
construct an evolutionary tree for
human populations.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.570
When we look at this tree, we see
a very clear division into Africans,

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.870
shown in red, and non-Africans,

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.200
shown in other colors, where each color
represents a different continent.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.040
But we also see that the most recent
common ancestor of all non-Africans is

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.620
at a much more recent point than the most
recent common ancestor of all Africans,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.710
which is at the root of the tree.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.270
In 1987 Rebecca Cann, Mark Stoneking and
Allan Wilson deduced from this information

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:40.130
that because the origin of modern humans
predates the origin of all non Africans,

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.540
all non-Africans must have
originated somewhere in Africa.

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.080
This became known as
the out-of-Africa hypothesis.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.760
And although it is still
the subject of some debate,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.300
it has become widely accepted today.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.060
As more genotyping data has been obtained,
researchers have been able

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:01.625
to infer human migrations around
the world from evolutionary trees.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.490
Yet this is not to say that genetic
data immediately is able to answer

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.300
all of our questions.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:15.640
One problem arises when different genetic
data sets yield differing phylogenies.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:21.160
For example,
using the dopamine D4 receptor gene

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.880
suggested that chimpanzees were closer
to gorillas than they were to humans.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.860
On the other hand, if we used
the beta-globin gene, that suggested that

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.680
chimpanzees were closer to humans
than they were to gorillas.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.830
Not until 1996 did enough
genetic data become available

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.290
to conclude that the phylogeny on
the right is most likely the correct one.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.020
Even at the beginning of this century,
many biologists believed

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:52.000
that humans are more closely related to
dogs than humans are related to mice.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:52.940
This seems reasonable,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:58.420
since we seem to be more similar
to dogs than mice at first glance.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.800
But recent research has shown otherwise,
as indicated by this phylogeny.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.010
Finally, evolutionary trees constructed

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.540
from genetic analysis have even been
used as evidence in criminal trials.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.600
A landmark instance of this occurred in
1998, when a doctor in Louisiana went on

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.270
trial for the attempted murder
of his estranged mistress.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:23.866
She claimed that he had injected
her with an HIV-tainted syringe.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:29.820
After the police chief collected
HIV samples from around the area,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.980
researchers constructed an evolutionary
tree for these samples.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:36.170
And what they found was that
the HIV samples from the victim,

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:37.700
shown here in blue,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.550
were similar to samples taken from one
of the doctor's patients, shown in red.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.060
This evidence supports conviction,
which was the eventual verdict.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.020
But this evolutionary tree taken
by itself is far from conclusive,

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.510
which is what brings us to
the challenge problem for this chapter.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.470
The evolutionary tree that we
constructed on the previous slide

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:04.150
was constructed based on an alignment
of just one HIV protein.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.140
What happens when you construct
alignments for other HIV proteins?

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.390
Do they all support conviction?

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:13.900
Or do some of these other
proteins paint a murkier picture?

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.130
So I will let you go and
work on implementing the algorithms for

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:22.200
evolutionary tree construction that
we have encountered in this chapter.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.349
I want to say thank you for
joining me on this journey.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.470
I can only hope that this chapter
has been as enjoyable for

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:28.780
you as it has been for me.

